/ActualText (Four panels depict matrices A, P, A*, and A' connected by core sampling, inverse probability, and decomposition processes. The figure shows four interconnected panels depicting the process of matrix factorization. The first panel, "Optimal Prob.," presents matrix A as a grid of varying gray squares. An arrow leads to "Core Sampling," influenced by matrix P, which is displayed below. Matrix P is defined by the formula P_ij = |A_ij| / f_i g_j. Next, "Inverse Prob." illustrates the resulting matrix A*, represented as another gray grid. An arrow connects A* to the final panel, "Decomposition," which shows matrix A' and relates to the formula A*_ij in {f_i g_j, 0}. The entire arrangement is enhanced with arrows and annotations, illustrating the flow of computational processes among the panels.)
/A 486 0 R
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/S /Figure
/T ()
/Pg 18 0 R
/Alt (Four panels depict matrices A, P, A*, and A' connected by core sampling, inverse probability, and decomposition processes. The figure shows four interconnected panels depicting the process of matrix factorization. The first panel, "Optimal Prob.," presents matrix A as a grid of varying gray squares. An arrow leads to "Core Sampling," influenced by matrix P, which is displayed below. Matrix P is defined by the formula P_ij = |A_ij| / f_i g_j. Next, "Inverse Prob." illustrates the resulting matrix A*, represented as another gray grid. An arrow connects A* to the final panel, "Decomposition," which shows matrix A' and relates to the formula A*_ij in {f_i g_j, 0}. The entire arrangement is enhanced with arrows and annotations, illustrating the flow of computational processes among the panels.)
>>
